#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "d97c3cf2-b627-5274-853a-be3d7e5cda40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10246" :question/problem "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcd6d6800caf6abdca868bba4abff3935f26e984807f13c25d4368dc858f2c1.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-6+8) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{4,-4,2\\} = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "b28b6939-847d-51ab-a97d-4367323d0d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-10247" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9, 3$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6]$ given that $f(6) = 2.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab98f2f27ff11ddb40138bf09900a865ca006bc8f26c95dfe03ea17fd4e49387.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-6,6),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-5) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(0) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(3-5) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-6}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-9+3-5) \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{2, 7, 4, 13\\} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = 2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "71cc1f65-437f-5928-82c2-be3dc4b0985f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10248" :question/problem "![](images/69/69d2d66022521424f9c6cda6ad901eb9ed1dbdb01044f5fa2d2954049770a3d4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,4].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9,$ $7$ and $8,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]$ given that $f(1) = 10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-7) \\\\  & =10+7 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-9-7) \\\\  & =10-(-16) \\\\  & =10+16 \\\\  & =26 \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10+8 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{10,17,26,18\\} = 26.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{max} = 26,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is  $x_\\text{max} = -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "708db737-4215-5df0-8940-c9d17dbd2b5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10249" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $14$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ given that $f(3) = -2.$\n\n![](images/de/deb5d80fe70d31fe768a180f3afd8a6054eda8e9fc3d1d0cd52c696c9de47660.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-5,5),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-3,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-2) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-14 \\\\  & =-16 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-2+14) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{-2, -4, -16, -14\\} = -16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = -16,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9eca73a-1249-5ae1-b362-ffc094617276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185443} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b4cbdf-1577-5a6a-99f5-35e017e43b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185444} {:answer/id #uuid "1345e68d-d5bf-5260-bf4d-cd225fed96a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185445} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb6dbb3-fbbe-59d2-9fe0-e3e1bfcc09bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185446} {:answer/id #uuid "5e94f044-8c76-5981-9173-610f747c5013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185447}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d153408e-bf16-54d4-a353-ba2449e79d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185442}] :question/id #uuid "0ab229b6-ba1f-5ab4-bb83-4e18f639baa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104271" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[- 3, 3]$. The graph of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ given that $f(0) = 56$."}]}
